.g. a text-based document is converted to a token-based document and the\n750:         annotations should be added to the token-based document.\n751: \n752:         Args:\n753:             other: The document to add annotations from.\n754:             process_predictions: Whether to process predictions as well (default: True). If set to False,\n755:                 the predictions in the other document will be ignored.\n756:             override_annotations: A mapping from annotation field names to a mapping from\n757:                 annotation IDs to annotations. The effects are two-fold. First, adding any annotation\n758:                 field name as key has the effect that the field is expected to be already handled and\n759:                 no annotations will be added from the other document. Second, if a certain mapping\n760:                 old_annotation._id -> new_annotation is present in the mapping for a certain field,\n761:                 the new_annotation will be used anywhere where the old_annotation would have been\n762:                 referenced. This propagates along the annotation graph and can be useful if some\n763:                 annotations are modified, but all dependent annotations should be kept intact e.g.\n764:                 when converting a text-based document to token-based (or the other way around). See below\n765:                 for an example.\n766:             removed_annotations: A mapping from annotation field names to a set of annotation ids that\n767:                 are removed from the document. This is useful if e.g. the annotation base (the text)\n768:                 is split up and the annotations need to be split up as well or if conversion to a token base\n769:                 is performed, but some spans could not be converted, because of aligning issues, and, thus,\n770:                 should be removed. Similar as for entries in override_annotations, fields that are mentioned\n771:                 in removed