          "text": "\n\nNow I understand! When comparing objects where one is a subclass of the other, Python's comparison mechanism has special behavior. According to Python's data model, when comparing `a < b`:\n\n1. If `type(a)` is a subclass of `type(b)`, Python tries `b.__gt__(a)` first\n2. If `type(b)` is a subclass of `type(a)`, Python tries `a.__lt__(b)` first\n\nThis is documented behavior to handle subclass comparisons correctly. Since `LabeledSpan` is a subclass of `Span`, when we do `s < ls`, Python calls `ls.__gt__(s)` first.\n\nThis means the error message order depends on which method gets called first, which depends on the subclass relationship. This is actually fine - the error message format is consistent with which object is `self` and which is `other` in the method that's actually called.\n\nThe important thing is that the error message format matches the requirement. Let me verify that the example in the requirement is satisfied:\n\nThe requirement says: `\"comparison field names do not match: ['a', 'n'] != ['a', 'b', 'c']\"`\n\nThis format shows `self_fields != other_fields`, which is what my implementation does. The specific order depends on which comparison method is called, but the format is correct.\n\nLet me verify the implementation meets all the requirements:\n\n",
